{
  "image_name": "3cbac7c05e733858404ac72c08534514.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how HER-family receptor signaling at the cell membrane drives tumor growth and survival, and how multiple alterations can keep this network active. Several related receptors are shown, including HER1/EGFR, HER2, HER3, and HER4, with HER2 emphasized as a central signaling partner. Receptors appear to form dimers and activate intracellular pathways, while mucin and truncated or mutant receptor forms seem to modify receptor behavior at the membrane.\n\nDownstream, the main pathways converge on MAPK signaling (RAS–RAF–MEK–ERK) and PI3K–AKT–mTOR, leading to gene transcription in the nucleus and ultimately proliferation and survival. Additional input from integrins and receptor tyrosine kinases through FAK/SRC, as well as ERα and metabolic pathways, also feeds into transcriptional programs.\n\nRed X marks and mutation symbols indicate likely sites of treatment resistance or bypass signaling. These include HER2 mutation, a PIK3CA mutation, and low or lost PTEN, which would sustain PI3K signaling even if receptor-level inhibition occurs. A tumor immune infiltrate is also depicted, suggesting the surrounding immune microenvironment is part of the broader biology. Overall, the image conveys a highly interconnected, redundant signaling system that can evade single-point therapeutic blockade.",
